mm/page_reporting: a lower reporting order requests a cycle

Free page reporting learns of reportable pages one way: a page freed at or
above the reporting order requests a cycle. Pages already free stay where
they are until one does. Lowering `page_reporting_order` at runtime makes
every free run between the old order and the new one reportable at a
stroke, and nothing requests the cycle that would report them: on an idle
guest whose agent had just moved the order from 128 KiB runs to 32 KiB
after a trim, the fragments sat unreported through the minute the Mac was
measured over (1872 MiB against 1349 with the order left at 32 KiB
throughout, M5, 2026-09-21), until some later free happened to be large
enough to ask.

Setting the order requests a cycle. Before the device registers the request
finds no device and does nothing, as every other notification does then.

--- a/mm/page_reporting.c
+++ b/mm/page_reporting.c
@@ -16,11 +16,21 @@ unsigned int page_reporting_order = -1;
 
 static int page_order_update_notify(const char *val, const struct kernel_param *kp)
 {
+	int err;
+
 	/*
 	 * If param is set beyond this limit, order is set to default
 	 * pageblock_order value
 	 */
-	return  param_set_uint_minmax(val, kp, 0, MAX_PAGE_ORDER);
+	err = param_set_uint_minmax(val, kp, 0, MAX_PAGE_ORDER);
+	/* lighter: a lower order makes runs already on the free lists
+	 * reportable, and only a free at or above the order would ask for
+	 * the cycle that reports them. Ask here. Before a device registers
+	 * the request finds none and does nothing.
+	 */
+	if (!err)
+		__page_reporting_notify();
+	return err;
 }
 
 static const struct kernel_param_ops page_reporting_param_ops = {
